\section{The run log}{

\code{\link[=run_plan]{run_plan()}} replaces the run log at its start with a short log that says
that the run has not finished. A run that ends before the next log, for
example when R stops, leaves that log. \code{\link[=run_plan]{run_plan()}} also writes the full
log when it stops on failed analyses, and \code{\link[=make_outputs]{make_outputs()}} adds its files
to it in the same R session. Each run replaces the file. The log is GitHub-flavoured Markdown in ASCII, with
these sections in this order:
\itemize{
\item \code{When}: the date and the time of the start of the run.
\item \code{Run}: the result in one sentence and the scenarios of the run. Then the
run date of each other scenario on the disk, from its manifest. Then the
versions of R, pbn, plnr and cohort, and the number of workers.
\item \code{Failures}, only after a failure: the failed analyses with their errors,
the failed assertions, and the failed rules of \code{\link[=check_results]{check_results()}}.
\item \code{Scenarios}: the simulations and the analyses of each scenario.
\item \code{Data}: the cache and the rows of each data element.
\item \code{Exclusions}: the exclusion table of simulation 1 of each scenario.
\item \code{Assertions}: the count for each scenario, and its assertions. For more
than 30 assertions, the failed ones only.
\item \code{Output} and \code{Files}: the files of \code{\link[=make_outputs]{make_outputs()}} and the results
files, with their sizes.
\item \code{Timings}: the seconds of each data step, of each analysis kind, and of
each step of the main session.
}

The log holds counts, exclusion tables and the rows of \code{check.csv}. It
never holds the text of an error, because an error message can hold a
value of the data. These errors get a number in the log:
\itemize{
\item the error of each failed analysis or data step, one number for each
message,
\item the error of an \code{expect_} function that stopped,
\item the detail of each failed rule of \code{\link[=check_results]{check_results()}}, which can quote a
name of the project,
\item the error of \code{save_results()} itself, also in the checks of its
arguments,
\item the error of the output function of \code{\link[=make_outputs]{make_outputs()}}.
}

The file \verb{<version>_results/run_errors_gitexclude.md} holds the full text
of each error under its number. In a git repository, pbn writes it only
when git ignores it, as the line \verb{*gitexclude*} of \code{.gitignore} does.
Otherwise the log says that the file is missing. Each run deletes the old
file, and pbn writes it only when the run has an error.

A project string outside a table, for example a file name of
\code{\link[=make_outputs]{make_outputs()}}, is on one line as a code span. A string in a table cell
is on one line too. So a line end in a string cannot start a heading or a
list item.

Only the sections \code{When} and \code{Timings} change in every run. Two runs of the
same code on one day, with the same data and cache, give the same other
lines. So put \code{run.md} in git, and \verb{git diff} shows the real changes of a
run. \code{\link[=render_run]{render_run()}} renders the log to a PDF.

pbn measures each time with \code{proc.time()} in the R session that does the
work, also on a worker. A data step gets the time of its function. Data
from the cache gets the time of the read. An analysis gets the time of its
action function. No timing goes to the value of \code{\link[=run_plan]{run_plan()}}, a results
file, \code{check.csv} or a cache key.
}

\section{The files}{

git gets the results of each scenario as one file, \code{results.qs2}, and
never the results of each simulation. These are the files of
\verb{<version>_results/<scenario>/}:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{check.csv                  the assertions of expect_<scenario>()
results.qs2                the results, stacked for more than 1 simulation
results_gitexclude.qs2     the kinds of `gitexclude`, stacked the same way
sims_gitexclude/           only for more than 1 simulation
  sim_<kkkk>.qs2           the results of simulation <kkkk>
  sim_<kkkk>_gitexclude.qs2  the kinds of `gitexclude` of simulation <kkkk>
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

A scenario with 1 simulation has no folder \verb{sims_gitexclude/}. Its
\code{results.qs2} is the results object of its one simulation. For more
simulations, \code{results.qs2} holds the simulations stacked, with the rules
of the section Stacking. The name of \verb{sims_gitexclude/} holds
\code{gitexclude}, so the line \verb{*gitexclude*} of \code{.gitignore} keeps it out of
git. A clone of the repository thus has no results of one simulation.
\code{\link[=read_results]{read_results()}} with \code{sim} reads them.

Before it writes a scenario, \code{save_results()} deletes each file in its
\verb{sims_gitexclude/}, and prints one line when there was one. So no
simulation of an earlier run stays, for example after \code{sims} became
smaller. For a scenario that now has 1 simulation, it deletes the folder.

\code{save_results()} stops when a file or a folder that it deletes is still
there afterwards, for example because another program holds it open on
Windows. The error names the file.

pbn 2026.11.22 and older wrote the results of each simulation to
\verb{sim_<kkkk>/results.qs2}, and git tracked them. When \code{save_results()}
writes a scenario with more than 1 simulation, it deletes each folder
\verb{sim_<kkkk>/} of that scenario, and prints one line.
}

\section{Stacking}{

The stacked results hold every element of the results of simulation 1,
in the same order. These rules apply at each depth:
\itemize{
\item A data.frame gets a first column \code{sim}, and the rows of the simulations
in order. It keeps the class of simulation 1, so a data.table stays a
data.table.
\item A list is stacked element by element, and pbn matches the elements by
name. So each element of a list MUST have its own name.
\item Each other element becomes a list with one entry for each simulation,
named \code{sim_0001}, \code{sim_0002} and so on. This includes an atomic vector,
a matrix, a factor, a date and \code{NULL}.
}

Stacking stops when two simulations differ in the structure at one path,
and the error names the path and the two simulations. It also stops on a
data.frame with a column \code{sim}, because pbn adds that column.

The \code{manifest} is that of simulation 1. In it, \code{sim} and \code{n_rows_data}
are \code{NA}, \code{sims} is the number of simulations, and \code{stacked} is \code{TRUE}.
The columns \code{from_cache} and \code{cache_key} of \code{data_cache} are \code{NA}. The
counts \code{n_rows_before_exclusions} and \code{n_rows_after_exclusions} are \code{NA}
too. The element \code{n_rows_by_sim} holds the counts of each simulation. It
is a data.frame with one row for each simulation and data element. Its
columns are \code{sim}, \code{data}, \code{n_rows_data}, \code{n_rows_before_exclusions} and
\code{n_rows_after_exclusions}. The rule \code{no_row_per_person} of
\code{\link[=check_results]{check_results()}} reads it.
}

\section{Two files}{

\code{save_results()} writes the results of the kinds in \code{gitexclude} to
\code{results_gitexclude.qs2}, beside \code{results.qs2}, and to
\verb{sim_<kkkk>_gitexclude.qs2}, beside each \verb{sim_<kkkk>.qs2}. The section The
files shows them. \code{results.qs2} and \verb{sim_<kkkk>.qs2} hold \code{manifest},
\code{exclusions} and every other kind. Use \code{gitexclude} for a
kind with fitted models, imputations or one row for each person. The
project's \code{.gitignore} MUST hold the line \verb{*gitexclude*}, so that git
never tracks that file. \code{\link[=read_results]{read_results()}} reads both files.

These rules apply:
\itemize{
\item \code{save_results()} stops before it writes anything when a name in
\code{gitexclude} is not an analysis kind of \code{p}.
\item In a git repository, it also stops when git does not ignore
\code{results_gitexclude.qs2}.
\item \code{\link[=check_results]{check_results()}} checks \code{results.qs2} and \verb{sim_<kkkk>.qs2}, and not
the files of \code{gitexclude}.
\item The simulations of the two files are stacked apart, with the rules of
the section Stacking.
\item Without kinds in \code{gitexclude}, \code{save_results()} deletes an old
\code{results_gitexclude.qs2} of the scenario, and prints one line. The old
\verb{sim_<kkkk>_gitexclude.qs2} files go with the rest of
\verb{sims_gitexclude/}, see the section The files.
}
}

\examples{
home <- file.path(tempdir(), "pbn_save_example")
dir.create(file.path(home, "v01_functions"), recursive = TRUE)
dir.create(file.path(home, "data"))
writeLines("# The run script.", file.path(home, "v01_run.R"))
writeLines(
  c(
    "CFG <- list(scenarios = data.frame(scenario = c('real', 'null'), sims = 1))",
    "expect_null <- function(results) {",
    "  data.frame(assertion = 'one row', expected = 1,",
    "    observed = nrow(results$means), pass = nrow(results$means) == 1)",
    "}"
  ),
  file.path(home, "v01_functions", "_cfg.R")
)
env <- new.env()
pbn::initialize_project("v01", home, file.path(home, "data"), env = env)

scenarios <- pbn::scenarios(env$CFG)
p <- plnr::Plan$new()
pbn::add_data(p, "d", fn = function(scenario, sim) {
  return(data.frame(x = stats::rnorm(5)))
})
pbn::add_analyses(p, scenarios, name = "means", fn = function(data, argset) {
  return(data.frame(mean_x = mean(data$d$x)))
})
r <- pbn::run_plan(p)
pbn::save_results(r, p, scenarios)
names(pbn::read_results("v01", home = home))

unlink(home, recursive = TRUE)
}
